{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01146 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Diphenylpyraline is implicated in the treatment of the common cold through a series of indirect pathways, all leading to the disease. The drug is associated with urticaria, a condition that is itself linked to the common cold via the indications of pheniramine, phenyltoloxamine, and azatadine. These antihistamines are known to alleviate symptoms of the common cold, such as nasal congestion and sneezing, by acting on histamine receptors. The evidence suggests that Diphenylpyraline may exert a therapeutic effect on the common cold through its ability to induce the expression of antihistaminic agents, which in turn modulate the inflammatory response associated with the disease. The overlapping indications across the three paths highlight a potential mechanism where Diphenylpyraline could influence the body's response to the common cold by promoting the use of related antihistaminic drugs, thereby supporting a role in the treatment of the condition.",
